  "claim": "The drive byte has done nearly all it will to the orders by the setting the page rests it at. On the three overdrive-into-modulation types walked at five settings, 0.86 of the change from 0 to 127 in the whole order content is reached by 48, and the 79 settings above it move that content by 0.28 dB more. The three follow one path, normalised to their own ends, to within 0.010 of it at every inner setting. Asked as a prediction of the 7 types walked at two settings from 40 upward -- three distortions and the drive stages of four multi-effects -- the law bounds how far the orders may move between the two, and all 7 moved by no more than 0.04 dB, which is under the bound everywhere. How the byte maps to a gain in front of the curve was asked on 02 00 alone, by matching its orders against a ladder of the part's own volume, and there it is 01 10's law: every setting from 4 to 64 wants twenty times the log of the setting over 48, in decibels, to within what the ladder resolves, and setting 8, where a byte straight in decibels would want 6.8 dB more, wants -15.9 against the law's -15.6. How it maps on the other ten types is not read.",
