    "the_sentence_it_replaced": " How the byte maps to a gain in front of the curve is not read by orders alone and is left open.",
    "how_the_run_was_made": "Not as the queue entry wrote it but as 01 10's ladder was made, which is what the entry asked to be matched: program 81 at note 45 rather than the held organ, the amp switch off and the chorus balance at 0, the drive byte walked from 0 to 127 at the volume the ladder's rung 100 gives and the volume walked from 16 to 127 at drive 48, with the part routed past the effect taken at every rung. The three settings the entry named are among those walked.",
    "the_rule": "Set before either record was read: the input setting 8 wants against 48, read off the ladder by the second order, is nearer one prediction than the other by more than the ladder places an input to at that level, measured by leaving each rung out and putting it back from the others; otherwise the run did not separate them.",
    "what_came_back": "Setting 8 wants -15.9 dB of input against 48. The law of 01 10 said -15.6 and is 0.3 off; a byte straight in decibels said -8.8 and is 7.1 off; the ladder places an input to 4.5 dB at that level, so the two distances differ by more than it resolves. Across the walk, against the rung it was made at: 4 wants -21.0 where the law says -21.6, a row good to 5.1; 8 wants -15.8 where the law says -15.6, a row good to 4.5; 12 wants -12.4 where the law says -12.0, a row good to 2.0; 16 wants -9.8 where the law says -9.5, a row good to 0.8; 24 wants -6.1 where the law says -6.0, a row good to 0.3; 32 wants -3.5 where the law says -3.5, a row good to 0.1; 48 wants +0.1 where the law says +0.0, a row good to 0.6; 64 wants +3.4 where the law says +2.5, a row good to 1.6.",
    "where_it_does_not_test": "Setting 2 matches an input where the ladder has already stopped moving, so the match there is to a place the ladder cannot tell from its neighbour and is no test; 0 has no gain to predict. Setting 4 agrees only as far as a row good to 5.1 dB can. From 80 up no rung returns a setting's orders -- the nearest misses by 12 to 49 dB of input -- and the ladder stops moving from above at -48.4 dBFS, as it did on 01 10, so no input the part's volume can give reaches those settings and they are not pinned to its last rung.",
    "where_the_two_runs_meet": "The two runs meet at one state: drive 48 on the walk and the volume ladder's rung 100 were made with the same bytes held and return the same orders to within both runs' repeats, -40.11 and -40.09 dBFS heard. Every figure is an order under the first order of its own take, so a gain after the stage does not reach it and settings of different loudness compare.",
